**Future** vs **CompletableFuture** — dono async result handle karte hain, but power level kaafi different hai.

---

**1. Future (basic async result)**

Limitations:

* Blocking (`get()`)
* No chaining
* No callback
* Hard to combine multiple tasks

Example

```java id="s3t0m7"
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(() -> {
    Thread.sleep(1000);
    return 10;
});

// blocking call
Integer result = future.get();

System.out.println(result);

executor.shutdown();
```

---

**2. CompletableFuture (modern async)**

Features:

* Non-blocking
* Chaining (thenApply, thenAccept)
* Combine multiple tasks
* Exception handling
* Callback style

Example

```java id="h7k1xq"
CompletableFuture<Integer> future =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 10)
        .thenApply(x -> x * 2)
        .thenApply(x -> x + 5);

future.thenAccept(System.out::println);
```

---

**3. Parallel combination**

```java id="8sxu9h"
CompletableFuture<Integer> f1 =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 10);

CompletableFuture<Integer> f2 =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 20);

CompletableFuture<Integer> result =
    f1.thenCombine(f2, (a,b) -> a + b);

System.out.println(result.join());
```

---

**4. Exception handling**

```java id="33hv5r"
CompletableFuture<Integer> future =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
        if(true) throw new RuntimeException();
        return 10;
    }).exceptionally(ex -> 0);

System.out.println(future.join());
```

---

**5. Key Differences**

| Feature            | Future        | CompletableFuture |
| ------------------ | ------------- | ----------------- |
| Blocking           | Yes (`get()`) | No (callbacks)    |
| Chaining           | ❌             | ✅                 |
| Combine tasks      | ❌             | ✅                 |
| Exception handling | Limited       | Powerful          |
| Manual completion  | ❌             | ✅                 |
| Functional style   | ❌             | ✅                 |

---

**6. Manual completion (only CompletableFuture)**

```java id="v6rxin"
CompletableFuture<String> future =
        new CompletableFuture<>();

new Thread(() -> {
    try {
        Thread.sleep(1000);
        future.complete("Done");
    } catch(Exception e) {
        future.completeExceptionally(e);
    }
}).start();

System.out.println(future.join());
```

---

**Interview one-liner**

```text id="a9qkmp"
Future is a simple blocking mechanism to retrieve async results,
while CompletableFuture provides non-blocking, composable, and functional APIs for async programming.
```

---

**Real-world tip (important)**

```text id="h7p5dx"
Always prefer CompletableFuture in modern Java (Java 8+)
unless you just need a simple blocking result.
```

---

