Java **TreeMap** me **hash collision ka concept hota hi nahi**. Collision ka concept **HashMap** me hota hai.

**Reason:**
`TreeMap` internally **Red-Black Tree** use karta hai, hashing nahi.

---

**TreeMap working (simplified):**

```java
TreeMap<K,V> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");
map.put(5, "C");
```

Internally structure:

```
        10
       /  \
      5    20
```

Ordering based on:

```java
1. Comparable (natural ordering)
or
2. Comparator (custom ordering)
```

---

**Key comparison logic**

```java
if (comparator != null)
    comparator.compare(k1, k2);
else
    ((Comparable)k1).compareTo(k2);
```

---

**Duplicate key handling**

```java
TreeMap<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(10, "A");
map.put(10, "B");

System.out.println(map);
```

Output

```
{10=B}
```

Because internally:

```java
if(compare(key, existingKey) == 0){
    replaceValue();
}
```

---

**Internal Node structure (simplified)**

```java
static final class Entry<K,V> {
    K key;
    V value;
    Entry<K,V> left;
    Entry<K,V> right;
    Entry<K,V> parent;
    boolean color; // RED or BLACK
}
```

---

**Contrast with HashMap collision**

HashMap me collision resolution:

```
Bucket -> LinkedList -> Tree (Java 8+)
```

Pseudo logic:

```java
if(bucketEmpty)
    insertNode();
else if(hashCollision)
    addToLinkedList();

if(listSize > 8)
    convertToRedBlackTree();
```

---

**Interview one-liner**

```
TreeMap does not handle hash collisions because it does not use hashing.
It stores entries in a Red-Black Tree and orders keys using Comparable or Comparator.
```

